WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.550
The DC Universe is broken.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:03.550
Superman is lost.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.980
Black Adam stayed in the red. Cyborg has been
silenced.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.300
Wonder Woman has vanished into thin air.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.170
And the Flash has gotten into so much trouble
that DC wishes he'd just run his way into

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:13.960
an entirely new timeline.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.260
But there's still hope for the future, my
friends.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.400
And the key to solving all of it?

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.174
Cartoons

00:00:22.011 --> 00:00:23.420
Hello Internet!

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:28.039
Welcome to Film Theory, the show that wants
you to subscribe faster than the Flash.

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:31.840
Bet you can't do it and write your favorite
superhero down in the comments in less than

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.641
3 seconds.

00:00:32.641 --> 00:00:33.840
Three, two.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.840
Wait, you did it?

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.059
Dang, you are faster than a speeding bullet.

00:00:37.059 --> 00:00:37.806
Well done.

00:00:37.806 --> 00:00:41.120
Now, it might be difficult to remember, but
there was a time not too long ago when DC

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.460
was the top dog when it came to superhero
movies.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.210
Remember The Dark Knight trilogy?

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.760
Those movies made over $2.4 billion at the
box office.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:52.350
One of them was so good that when it wasn't
nominated for best picture at the Oscars,

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:57.063
the Academy literally changed their rules
to avoid anything like that ever happening again.

00:00:57.063 --> 00:00:58.087
But in the following years…

00:00:58.087 --> 00:00:58.888
Yeah…

00:00:58.888 --> 00:01:00.093
Yeah. Not so much.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:06.102
The DCEU, which officially started with Man
of Steel ten years ago, has been an absolute mess.

00:01:06.102 --> 00:01:08.680
And that's not meant to be a judgment call
or measure of quality.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:13.530
I mean, just as a point of fact, right now
there are three different Batmen and two separate

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:16.850
Jokers running around at the same time, and
that's just the tip of the iceberg.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:22.189
Since the DCEU started, it's been a nightmare
of hiring, firing and rehiring multiple directors

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:23.619
for the same project.

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:26.840
Actors being let go brought back again, only
to be let go again.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:31.270
There's been public fallout over bad behavior
by studio executives, constant cameos and

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:36.204
teases for films that will never happen while
completely finished movies are just outright canceled.

00:01:36.204 --> 00:01:39.979
Meanwhile, you got star actors dealing with
felony allegations popping up like the worst

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:41.380
game of whack a mole ever.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.690
And the only thing that's dropped further
than the quality of some of these things is

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:48.960
WB's stock price, which got so low that it
was basically sold for spare parts to the

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.630
guy who runs the Discovery Channel.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.119
Gotta be honest here your loyal theorists,
as someone who grew up loving DC’s heroes

00:01:54.119 --> 00:01:57.560
more than Marvel's, it's been really heartbreaking
to see this.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.119
But now there's new hope.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:02.869
Warner Brothers has brought on James Gunn,
writer and director of Guardians of the Galaxy,

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:07.958
The Suicide Squad and Peacemaker, to shepherd
their movies back to financial and critical success.

00:02:07.958 --> 00:02:11.550
And, while he certainly has a long path ahead
of him to get the DC Universe back to where

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:12.550
it needs to be.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.791
I think he can do it, provided he follows
my advice today.

00:02:15.791 --> 00:02:17.110
That's right, loyal theorists.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:20.989
Today I plan to give this new master of the
Universe a five point guide on how to make

00:02:20.989 --> 00:02:23.580
a successful DC cinematic franchise.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:24.900
And I'm not just pulling this from anywhere.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.460
I have proof that these steps will work.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.800
So put on your super suits and kiss your Martha's
goodbye.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.069
Let's save ourselves a franchise, shall we?

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:37.599
Hey there theorists, future MatPat here, for
the second Film Theory in a row go figure.

00:02:37.599 --> 00:02:41.120
So literally, just a couple of hours ago,
as I write this, James Gunn came out and did

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.220
a big presentation talking about DC's future
plans, announcing ten new projects for Gods

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.050
and Monsters, the so-called first chapter
of their big DC reboot.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:53.260
Mind you, this is a couple of days after we
wrote and recorded this and got the audio

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.460
out to our editors.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.349
James Gunn's announcement kinda came out of
nowhere.

00:02:56.349 --> 00:03:00.010
Usually they preface this sort of stuff or
try to time it around big industry events,

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:03.800
but nope, this was him just dropping a big
ol’ promotional bomb in the middle of everyone.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.890
So what I'm going to be doing now is laying
out my advice exactly as it was written, not

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.159
changing the script at all.

00:03:09.159 --> 00:03:12.900
But then at the end of each section, I'm going
to pop back in to assess how Gunn seems to

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.310
be handling things going forward.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:15.870
Does it look like he's taking our advice?

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:20.350
Is he ignoring it and doing his own thing,
or is he falling into the mistakes of his predecessors?

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.690
Let's just jump back into the theory and find
out.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:27.190
Step number one, take care of your heavy hitters,
both in terms of characters as well as the

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:28.280
talent behind them.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.330
In pop culture, DC’s characters have a pretty
strong brand identity.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:35.880
Most people know who Superman, Batman, Wonder
Woman, Aquaman and the Flash are.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.129
Heck, even Green Lantern.

00:03:37.129 --> 00:03:41.099
So at least to start, your focus needs to
be on revitalizing most of the core cast with

00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:42.200
strong movies or series.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.959
It’s not to say that I don't think a Cyborg
or a Black Adam or a Birds of Prey can work

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:46.959
on screen.

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:50.709
But why would people go to see those if you
can't even get Superman right?

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:53.250
Right now, DC needs to rebuild the audience's
trust.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:57.640
They need to let them know that they're delivering
fun, high quality movies consistently with

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.819
all the name brands that we know and love.

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:03.000
Then and only then will people start to come
out for the second stringers.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.599
I mean, case in point, just look at Marvel
phase one.

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:09.680
You got to do your Captain Americas and Thor's
justice before anyone gives the talking tree

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.329
and sassy rabbit a chance.

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:14.010
The Marvel brand can carry smaller characters
now.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.726
All that said, the other side of the coin
is after you've done a few of your core characters justice.

00:04:18.726 --> 00:04:22.816
If a creative comes along and has an amazing
pitch for something more obscure, run with it.

00:04:22.816 --> 00:04:26.753
You may end up making C-list characters like
Animal Man or the Doom Patrol into household names.

00:04:26.753 --> 00:04:29.100
And we know that DC is capable of this.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.280
Look no further than Shazam.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.100
It had a great story with a likable tone and
a strong cast, and now you're seeing him enter

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.039
the cultural zeitgeist more and more.

00:04:36.039 --> 00:04:39.400
Even more obscure; before The Suicide Squad,
no one had ever heard of a Peacemaker.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.880
But now he's played by a world famous wrestler
and has his own popular TV show.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.990
My mom has heard of Peacemaker.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:49.340
Again, Marvel has routinely done this when
they didn't have the rights to their biggest

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:50.900
names like Spider-Man or the X-Men.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.560
They looked at their second stringers.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.520
The core Avengers cast used to be the guys
that cameoed in the Spider-Man cartoons.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.270
Now they're all huge franchises of their own
right.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.930
And I don't just mean Iron Man and Captain
America here either.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:05.530
They've turned the Scarlet Witch, Shang-Chi,
Moon Knight and the Guardians of the Galaxy

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:09.790
into brands that are big enough to carry their
own films, TV shows and theme park rides.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:12.630
Kevin Feige has gone on record saying that
when they're deciding which characters to

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.760
adapt, it doesn't matter how famous or popular
the character is in the comics, only that

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.080
there's great potential for a movie.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.520
Story should always come first.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.122
All right.

00:05:21.122 --> 00:05:22.379
Future MatPat with the assessment.

00:05:22.379 --> 00:05:24.510
So how's Gunn handling things?

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.530
Well, it kind of remains to be seen.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:30.620
Though there was a big focus on projects like
the new iterations of the core cast with Superman

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.110
Legacy and the Brave and the Bold.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:36.160
There was also a surprisingly large amount
of obscure stuff thrown in the mix.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.160
Swamp Thing?

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.160
Booster Gold?

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.270
Still, I think Gunn understands that they
need to get their heavy hitters right, he

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.890
told Deadline in one of the interviews, Quote.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:47.211
“One of our strategies is that we take our
Diamond characters - Batman, Superman, Wonder

00:05:47.211 --> 00:05:51.150
Woman - and we use that to help prop up other
characters that people don't know.”

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.370
And thus we get ourselves Creature Commandos.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:06:00.000
So in theory, IN SUPERHERO THEORY! he seems
to get it, But we'll see how things pan out.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.370
Which leads me to my next piece of advice;
Number two: trust the source material.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:08.729
The fans love these characters and they have
loved these characters for decades.

00:06:08.729 --> 00:06:11.601
There's a reason for that, and it's your job
to understand why.

00:06:11.601 --> 00:06:15.030
What is the core value of that character to
the audience?

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.150
And then once you understand what that is,
do not change it.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:23.030
There are so many recent examples of people
using IP without any sort of respect for the

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:24.030
source material.

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:25.350
Isn't that right, Velma?

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.810
For instance, Superman, the symbol of truth,
justice and the American way.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:33.180
Should not be destroying whole city blocks
or snapping necks in a gritty drama.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:35.970
Or if he is, that needs to be the point.

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.010
That is the dramatic struggle.

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.940
That's the conflict that the movie has to
explore throughout its runtime.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:44.660
The character who is determined to be purely
good, is suddenly forced into positions where

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.490
he has to question his own ethical line.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.210
That? that is an interesting movie.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.490
It's the superhero version of the trolley
problem.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:54.620
Dig into that because it plays into what people
love about Superman.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.280
His unironic, unspoiled sense of pure goodness
and hope.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:01.349
Don't just leave it as some sort of footnote
to happen at the end of the film.

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:04.850
If you want an example of what not to do,
take a look at Netflix's The Witcher.

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:09.050
It started out great with Henry Cavill, a
beloved actor and fan of the material stepping

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.599
in to play the title character.

00:07:10.599 --> 00:07:14.599
But things quickly devolved as the creatives
started changing parts of the Witcher mythos,

00:07:14.599 --> 00:07:18.849
to the point that rumors started swirling
that the writers hated their source material.

00:07:18.849 --> 00:07:21.840
That right there is what we call a big red
flag.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.100
And the fan reviews for both Season two and
the spinoff Blood Origins reflect that.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.560
Is it any wonder that Henry Cavill bowed out
of the franchise that he loved so he could

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:31.340
go work on a Warhammer adaptation?

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:35.030
He even released a public statement saying,
(mis)quote, “I promise to respect this IP

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:36.030
that we love.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:37.710
I promise to bring you something familiar.”

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:42.180
Not a good look for the creative team over
at The Witcher, huh?

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.159
In short, audiences aren't dumb.

00:07:44.159 --> 00:07:46.560
They can tell when something's made with a
genuine love for the IP.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:49.940
So if you find out that your writer staff
can't be bothered to pick up and read some

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:52.050
comic books, then just stop what you're doing.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:55.919
Stop it right there and find a project that
they are excited about or, you know, scrap

00:07:55.919 --> 00:07:58.760
the whole team and get someone who actually
wants the project.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:00.720
You know, a character that has some ridiculous
lore?

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.360
Sonic the Hedgehog, But both Sonic movies
have embraced that video game weirdness to

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.560
deliver movies that are in the top ten highest
grossing for the year.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:09.210
They respected the source material.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:12.900
Speaking of that Sonic lore, by the way, if
you're interested in just how crazy Sonic

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.150
lore gets and how the newest game's release
breaks all of it.

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:18.599
We just released a Game Theory about that
very topic.

00:08:18.599 --> 00:08:20.530
Link is in the top right corner of the screen.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:24.620
Make sure you watch that one after we're done
solving the DCEU’s problems.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:28.380
Future MatPat back again, happy to say that
I think Gunn nails this point.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:32.149
The new Superman project seems to be borrowing
from more hopeful stories, the Brave and the

00:08:32.149 --> 00:08:36.719
Bold is bringing more of Batman’s very popular
family of characters onto the screen, and

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:39.380
embracing stranger parts of the obscure IP
they’re adapting, Awesome.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:44.130
Even that Supergirl movie is adapting a really
new, really weird story that fans and critics

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:47.610
loved, borrowing more from stuff like John
Carter than Smallville.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:51.120
On paper, everything here is looking great,
which makes a lot of sense.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.050
I mean, we’re talking about a guy who made
a talking racoon, alien tree, and Polka-Dot

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:56.350
Man work on film.

00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:59.839
If there was any one of these 5 points that
he was going to knock out of the park, it

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:00.839
was going to be this one.

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:01.839
Now back to the advice.

00:09:01.839 --> 00:09:04.899
Point number three and this is a bit of a
corollary to what we just talked about.

00:09:04.899 --> 00:09:08.779
If a character doesn't fit a tone, don't force
them into a movie with that tone.

00:09:08.779 --> 00:09:12.590
Basically, you don't want to try to ram a
Superman shaped block through a Batman shaped

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:16.540
hole, despite both of them being superheroes,
the types of stories they tell aren't anywhere

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:20.350
close to the same Something I don't think
a lot of people understand about Marvel's

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:22.710
output is that they aren't superhero movies.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:25.000
They're movies with superheroes in them. Sure.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.829
And they do have consistent factors.

00:09:26.829 --> 00:09:28.839
But “superhero” isn't the genre.

00:09:28.839 --> 00:09:32.220
Instead, Marvel looks at their characters
and then writes stories about them in genres

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:33.220
that make sense.

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:34.250
To really explain what I mean.

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:38.140
Let's just take a look at three of the franchises
Captain America, Ant-Man and Spider-Man.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:42.250
All three of these series have Marvel's trademark
quippy humor, costume design, art, direction,

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:43.250
all that jazz.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:45.460
But the types of stories they're telling are
all very different.

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:49.455
Winter Soldier and Civil War are political
thrillers making commentary on serious social issues.

00:09:58.984 --> 00:10:03.079
Both Ant-Man films are heist movies, featuring
Scott Lang breaking into somewhere highly

00:10:03.079 --> 00:10:05.462
secure to either steal something or rescue
someone.

00:10:11.209 --> 00:10:15.073
And both of these franchises are way different
from the Spider-Man movies, which are all

00:10:15.073 --> 00:10:17.685
Gen-Z, John Hughes, coming-of-age films.

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:28.519
The stakes are different, the structures are
different, the genres are different.

00:10:28.519 --> 00:10:29.980
So how does that apply to DC?

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:31.769
Well, Batman's historically done this well.

00:10:31.769 --> 00:10:34.310
The Christopher Nolan Batman trilogy are crime
thrillers.

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:38.190
Matt Reeves’s the Batman leaned into the
noir elements of a detective story.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:40.370
Both of them were dark and gritty and rightfully
so.

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:44.300
But the problem begins when you start to layer
that tone across a whole franchise.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:44.830
Wonder Woman?

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:45.330
Shazam?

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:46.071
Superman?

00:10:46.071 --> 00:10:49.480
They're not gritty characters, so trying to
layer them into gritty stories is going to

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:50.700
create tonal conflict.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:54.550
This was arguably one of the biggest issues
with the early phases of the DCEU.

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:58.170
Zack Snyder's aesthetic was being applied
like a filter over everything.

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:02.810
The genre of those early movies was “Superhero/comic-book”
and nothing really more.

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:05.358
But if you look at the movies in the franchise
that worked the best: Shazam!

00:11:05.358 --> 00:11:10.240
Wonder Woman, Aquaman and THE Suicide Squad,
they weren't afraid to break from the mold

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:11.860
and carve out their own tone.

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:15.690
Wonder Woman (the good one) was part period
war film, part mythological epic.

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:19.870
Aquaman borrowed a lot from old school adventure
movies like Indiana Jones and The Mummy, and

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:21.560
audiences loved both of those.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:23.940
Ocean Man movie has drum playing octopus?

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:24.940
Awesome!

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:26.431
Kids superhero movie has YouTube humor?

00:11:26.431 --> 00:11:27.105
Perfect!

00:11:27.105 --> 00:11:30.000
Talking shark movie has giant alien starfish
enemy?

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:30.726
Cool!

00:11:30.726 --> 00:11:34.550
Just because everyone in the DCEU shares the
collective world, it doesn't mean that everyone's

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:37.459
part in that world needs to share the same
tone.

00:11:37.459 --> 00:11:41.180
You need to look at the characters that you're
working with and then make movies around them,

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:43.970
not force them into the movies that you're
trying to make.

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:48.810
So make an upbeat Superman action film with
a journalistic subplot, make a space cop action

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:53.519
drama with the Green Lantern, just give them
all room to breathe and do their own thing.

00:11:53.519 --> 00:11:57.329
Future Matpat review time: DC’s approach
to this one seems perfect.

00:11:57.329 --> 00:12:00.790
Not only are they letting stuff like the Batman
and Joker sit in their own worlds with their

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:05.430
own tone, they’re also carrying over the
only parts of the DCEU that people loved;

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:08.100
Aquaman, Wonder Woman, Shazam, and The Suicide
Squad.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:12.139
Plus they are very clearly embracing different
tones in the new adaptations that they’re

00:12:12.139 --> 00:12:13.139
bringing to the table.

00:12:13.139 --> 00:12:16.660
They even had the same idea for that “True
Detective in Space” version of Green Lantern

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.460
we just pitched right there.

00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:20.139
Great minds thinking alike, huh James?

00:12:20.139 --> 00:12:22.680
Piece of advice number four, And this is a
big one: Take your time.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:24.110
Take your time making the movies good.

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:24.676
Duh.

00:12:24.676 --> 00:12:28.370
But also, more importantly, take your time
announcing what you're working on.

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:31.899
That way you can see what works, what audiences
react positively to, and what you need to

00:12:31.899 --> 00:12:34.600
work on behind the scenes if something doesn't
pan out.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:36.530
All without publicly embarrassing everyone
involved.

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:37.531
Seems obvious, right?

00:12:37.531 --> 00:12:38.187
You would think.

00:12:38.187 --> 00:12:40.699
But a lot of studios make this mistake.

00:12:40.699 --> 00:12:44.630
See, the MCU had this weird marketing strategy
for a film franchise when it was still young.

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:48.420
They'd have Kevin Feige come out at ComicCon
and announce the entire next 3 to 5 years

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.660
of movies all at once.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:53.080
At the time when they were still growing their
brand, it worked really well for them.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.320
It got fans excited, which built up hype.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.160
But this taught the other studios the wrong
lessons.

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:01.139
Everyone wanted a piece of the pie and so
they tried their best to start the next MCU

00:13:01.139 --> 00:13:02.240
ASAP.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:05.870
Universal might just be the best, worst example
of this that I can think of with their attempted

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:11.100
reboot of their classic monster movies under
the Dark Universe banner back in the mid 2010s.

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:15.480
They announced half a dozen movies mapped
out with a deep mythology's worth of lore.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:19.720
They cast Hollywood superstars like Tom Cruise,
Johnny Depp, Angelina Jolie.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:23.970
They even released a star studded cast photo
of the major players all in one place.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:25.899
So you knew that they were serious about it.

00:13:25.899 --> 00:13:29.680
Fun fact, by the way, none of those actors
were actually in the same room for this photoshoot.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:31.040
They were just Photoshopped together.

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:33.790
Anyway, the one thing that Universal didn't
do in all of this?

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.750
Make sure the movies that launched the franchise
were actually worth watching.

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:41.160
Dracula Untold was a moderate financial success,
but was eviscerated by critics and fans.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:43.250
And The Mummy, starring Tom Cruise?

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:44.250
It did even worse.

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:47.930
The most memorable thing about that movie
was the trailer they mistakenly uploaded without

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:48.915
any audio.

00:13:55.222 --> 00:13:59.360
Yep. That was a real trailer uploaded by a real
major movie studio.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:03.490
All in all, even with the exciting IP and
awesome star power they had lined up, Universal

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:06.800
did not take their time building up this IP,
and as a result, people looked at the Dark

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:10.339
Universe and said:
I'm not interested in that, at all.

00:14:10.339 --> 00:14:11.709
DC did the exact same thing.

00:14:11.709 --> 00:14:12.709
In 2014.

00:14:12.709 --> 00:14:16.519
They announced ten movies for their new universe,
spearheaded by Zack Snyder, ranging from Wonder

00:14:16.519 --> 00:14:19.000
Woman and Aquaman to Shazam! and Cyborg.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.940
Some were as far away as six years.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:24.784
The only problem with this, they didn't actually
wait to see how people liked the first couple of movies.

00:14:24.784 --> 00:14:29.170
Cue the internal executives Scramble studio
interference, replacing directors, shifting

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:32.029
schedules, delays, cancellations and public
embarrassment.

00:14:32.029 --> 00:14:35.459
All of them were self-inflicted wounds that
were totally avoidable.

00:14:35.459 --> 00:14:37.870
Warner Brothers didn't need to go out and
announce ten movies.

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:41.680
They could have just gone with three or four,
seen how people liked the first stab at things

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:45.080
and then privately changed plans in the background
when it became clear that audiences weren't

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:46.230
clicking with the DCEU.

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:48.899
This is the one that makes future MatPat a
little worried.

00:14:48.899 --> 00:14:53.240
I'm skeptical about the fact that so many
projects just got announced this week, another

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:58.579
ten in total, which is just like that initial
wave of DCEU announcements.

00:14:58.579 --> 00:15:01.570
That being said, I suppose they're not completely
starting from scratch here.

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:05.779
They are bringing what worked from the DCEU
over into this new universe, so at least they're

00:15:05.779 --> 00:15:07.100
starting on a positive note.

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:10.600
Plus, I also found this quote where Gunn said
that they're willing to delay movies if the

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:11.920
scripts aren't 100% ready.

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:15.970
Now, I would hope that this would normally
be the default in Hollywood, but looking at

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:18.700
blockbusters over the last decade, clearly
not.

00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:22.249
But I wouldn't be getting too far ahead of
myself because piece of advice number five:

00:15:22.249 --> 00:15:23.439
Don't have a long term plan.

00:15:23.439 --> 00:15:24.295
I’m not kidding.

00:15:24.295 --> 00:15:25.585
Don't meticulously plan out the new universe.

00:15:25.585 --> 00:15:27.910
So you know exactly what's supposed to happen
and when.

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:31.230
I know it sounds counterproductive, but I
promise that you'll regret doing this.

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:33.089
By having so much of your franchise laid out.

00:15:33.089 --> 00:15:34.740
You don't give yourself room to maneuver.

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:38.411
If something changes, if a new character pops
up that you want to insert into the franchise

00:15:38.411 --> 00:15:41.800
or a writer comes along to offer you a great
story that otherwise doesn't fit.

00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:43.529
Again, you can learn from Marvel here.

00:15:43.529 --> 00:15:46.330
And the big example I'm going to point to
is Captain America Civil War.

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:48.690
This was a huge movie for the franchise.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:52.750
Basically Avengers 2.5, featuring the culmination
of a ton of stories and setting up a ton of

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:53.800
others for the future.

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:56.079
From the outside looking in, it might have
seemed like they planned the whole thing from

00:15:56.079 --> 00:15:57.420
the start, but they didn't.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:59.550
Civil War wasn't even the original pitch.

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:03.820
The writers wanted to adapt a different Captain
America story called MadBomb, but Feige didn't

00:16:03.820 --> 00:16:04.980
think that it went big enough.

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:06.500
So things pivoted to Civil War.

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:10.520
And even then there were multiple versions
of Civil War floating around with and without

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:11.520
Spider-Man.

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:14.949
Since Marvel was busy negotiating over the
character with Sony, and if they hadn't come

00:16:14.949 --> 00:16:15.970
to an agreement there?

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:19.750
Ant-Man would have switched sides to become
Team Iron Man, thereby changing his entire

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:21.870
character arc in all the future movies.

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:25.610
But Marvel just decided to go with the flow
and did what worked in the moment.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:28.880
Now, that's not to say that you shouldn't
have a vague idea of what to do or where your

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:29.880
story is going.

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:32.500
You 100% should, so you can build to it.

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:37.000
Marvel did exactly that in Hulk and Iron Man
1, teasing the Avengers initiative because

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.960
they knew that eventually they were going
to have a crossover.

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:42.089
They just didn't have what was going to happen
in The Avengers laid out.

00:16:42.089 --> 00:16:46.449
And then the Avengers movie itself teased
Thanos, because again, Marvel knew that eventually

00:16:46.449 --> 00:16:50.300
they'd want him to be a big bad, but writing
out how exactly they were going to get their

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:52.160
beat for beat? That's no bueno.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:56.569
Seriously, did you know that for a while nobody
at Marvel had decided where the Infinity Stones

00:16:56.569 --> 00:16:59.480
were? Or even what was and wasn't an Infinity
stone.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:16:59.980
True story.

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:03.399
According to tweets from James Gunn himself,
when they first shot Guardians of the Galaxy,

00:17:03.399 --> 00:17:07.089
the Infinity Stone in the movie was Red, the
color of the reality stone.

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:11.390
But in post it had to be changed to the Purple
Power stone because Marvel had decided after

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:15.195
the fact that the red one would be coming
from Thor the Dark Worlds aether.

00:17:15.195 --> 00:17:18.630
Heck! They didn't even know why Thanos wanted the Stones to kill half the universe until they

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:20.830
sat down to write the script for Infinity
War.

00:17:20.830 --> 00:17:25.360
I mean, if you need any more proof of their
lack of a plan, they featured the Infinity

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:28.380
Gauntlet in Odin's vault as early as phase
one.

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:29.403
But plans changed…

00:17:30.433 --> 00:17:34.250
And it was retconned, which, when you think
about it, is kind of funny because this then

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:37.910
is a fake of a gauntlet that hadn't even been
built yet.

00:17:42.753 --> 00:17:43.551
But you know what?

00:17:43.551 --> 00:17:46.059
I don't hate it because we got a better movie
franchise out of it.

00:17:46.059 --> 00:17:48.440
Final future MatPat review: I'm not too worried
there.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:51.810
Even though they did talk about plans to have
characters appear in multiple projects, Gunn

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:55.850
didn't come out and announce a giant crossover
event that everything's leading up to.

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:59.888
It seems like they're just going to go with
the flow for now, which I think is the smart option.

00:17:59.888 --> 00:18:04.660
That said, there is still one final big thing
that I want to point out to Gunn here.

00:18:04.660 --> 00:18:06.540
So that is a lot of advice.

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:07.930
But here's the biggest twist of them all.

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:12.630
DC already has the perfect blueprint to model
parts of this new cinematic universe from,

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:15.050
and it's followed basically all of this advice.

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:17.830
It was called the DC Animated Universe.

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:21.780
If you grew up with DC in the nineties and
aughts, you definitely recognize this franchise.

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:25.500
It's where a lot of the iconic modern portrayals
of DC characters originated.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.390
Kevin Conroy's Batman, Mark Hamill's Joker.

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:32.190
Those were these versions of Batman and Joker
before they made their way into the animated

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:33.590
films and Arkham video games.

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:36.780
So how does the DCAU follow my advice?

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:37.990
Let's go point by point.

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:40.490
Firstly, yes, they knew that the heavy hitters
were important.

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:44.669
They started this universe out with Batman
and then they grew into a Superman show before

00:18:44.669 --> 00:18:47.020
they had the two of them team up in Justice
League.

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:48.020
But what else did they do?

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:51.740
Well, an awesome new character named Static
came along in the comics and they jumped at

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:55.390
the chance to adapt him with a creative team
that were excited to tell his stories.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:19:00.000
And when the really weird but really cool
idea for a cyberpunk Batman spin off came along?

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:01.549
They made Batman Beyond.

00:19:01.549 --> 00:19:04.621
They also let less important characters shine
in the Justice League shows.

00:19:04.621 --> 00:19:05.205
Seriously.

00:19:05.205 --> 00:19:10.110
Hawkgirl, The Question, Etrigan the Demon,
all minor characters that had leading roles,

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:12.690
episodes, even entire story arcs devoted to
them.

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:16.320
Not only that, but they adapted these characters
in ways that didn't pigeonhole them into tones

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:17.320
that didn't fit.

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:19.600
They let Batman have a darker, more noir tone.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:24.150
Superman was more upbeat and sci fi, Static
Shock didn't feel the same as Batman Beyond,

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:26.250
which also didn't feel the same as Justice
League.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:28.380
Fourthly, they took their time building the
world.

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:31.470
Batman The Animated Series, was first released
in 1993.

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:35.880
Superman didn't get his spin off until 1996,
and Justice League didn't come along until

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:36.880
2001.

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:41.360
And finally, though DCAU head honcho Bruce
Timm might have had ideas on where to take

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:45.200
the universes story the creators didn't plan
beyond what seasons and shows had already

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:46.200
been greenlit.

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:49.530
They didn't go into Batman in the early nineties,
mapping out where their Justice League spin

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:52.000
off in 2005 was going to take them.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:56.490
Listen, DC James, you guys have a golden opportunity
here to take some of the most beloved characters

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:59.010
and IP in the world and make something really
meaningful.

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:02.640
It won't be easy, but exactly how to do it
is right in front of you.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:04.150
All you need to do is follow my advice.

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:06.810
And just go watch some great old cartoons.

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:10.490
In the meantime, though, if you want to see
my last piece of advice that I gave to DC

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:13.760
that they chose not to follow, check out my
theory on how they should have used the Snyder

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:16.460
cut to fix the DCEU timeline.

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:19.970
Or if you want to see what Batman villain
DC should have set up with The Batman.

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:22.560
Check out my theory about Hush and the Joker.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.549
Don't forget to subscribe for more superhero
theories coming your way soon.

00:20:25.549 --> 00:20:28.284
And as always, remember, it's all just a theory.

00:20:28.284 --> 00:20:30.000
A FILM THEORY!

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.833
Aaaaaaaaaand cut.

